\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Yes \'97 
\f1\b with continuous conservation, not untouched preservation
\f0\b0 . That distinction gives you plenty of room for high-jinks.\
A 1950s Cadillac is actually a better thousand-year survivor than many later cars because so much of the important structure is metal, glass and fairly substantial mechanical engineering. The body, chassis, engine block, transmission casing, chrome, glass and assorted fittings could plausibly reach 3012 if generations of custodians kept corrosion under control. But rubber, plastics, adhesives, leather, upholstery, seals, hoses, belts, tyres, wiring insulation, lubricants and fluids are the enemy. Museum guidance already treats rubber and organic vehicle components as consumables or things that may need removal and replica replacement; even under proper indoor conditions, temperature, humidity, UV, gravity and corrosion all need continual management. ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.canada.ca/en/conservation-institute/services/preventive-conservation/guidelines-collections/vehicles.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Canada}})\
So I would 
\f1\b not
\f0\b0  give Ulyssia miraculous thousand-year-old tyres. Give it a religiously conserved Cadillac of Theseus.\
Something like:\

\f1\b 97.3% authentic by legally recognised Elvisian mass.
\f0\b0 \
Its chassis, major body panels, engine castings and perhaps much of the trim are original. Everything perishable has been replaced hundreds of times. This creates vicious theological arguments over whether it is still 
\f2\i the
\f0\i0  Cadillac.\
And Elvis gives you an excellent historical specimen. His famous surviving pink car is the 
\f1\b 1955 Cadillac Fleetwood Series 60
\f0\b0 , now at Graceland. It has already survived about seventy years as a museum object. More importantly for your purposes, it wasn't even Elvis's first pink Cadillac: the earlier one 
\f1\b caught fire in June 1955
\f0\b0 , after its brake lining caught fire while he was returning from a gig.\
That almost demands recurrence.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::3>
\f1\b\fs28 The preservation itself can have become ritual\
