\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 Actual museum practice contains things that would become beautifully ridiculous after a millennium.\
For example, vehicle conservators recommend taking the weight off tyres and wheels with axle supports, or periodically rotating wheels to avoid permanent deformation. ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.canada.ca/en/conservation-institute/services/preventive-conservation/guidelines-collections/vehicles.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Canada}})\
By 3012 this could have become:\

\f1\b The Turning of the Wheels
\f0\b0 , an Elvisian holy observance performed every eight years.\
Nobody remembers why.\
The High Mechanic chants the prescribed sequence while four acolytes rotate each wheel exactly one-eighth of a revolution. The theological schism concerns whether the left rear wheel is turned before or after the recitation of 
\f2\i Viva Las Vegas
\f0\i0 .\
The original conservation manual survives, but only as scripture, badly translated through nine hundred years:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 \strokec2 Raise vehicles and rotate wheels regularly.
\f0\i0 \strokec2 \
has become a commandment against allowing Cadillacs to remain facing east.\
And the museum advice to 
\f1\b slacken drive belts during storage
\f0\b0  is even better. ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.canada.ca/en/conservation-institute/services/conservation-preservation-publications/canadian-conservation-institute-notes/rubber-components-industrial-collections.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Canada}})\
Imagine a sect called the 
\f1\b Slack Belters
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

